ssure  here self-isolation is not an option  people in nagorno-karabakh are trapped  between the visible  and invisible threats to their lives  i'm joined now by richard garagosian  he's the head of the regional studies  center  a foreign policy think tank in yerevan  armenia  richard welcome to dw um can you confirm  what isai baijan claims which is  that the missile strikes on ganja were a  deliberate attack by the armenian army  actually no i can't but i can begin with  deploring the indiscriminate violence  the loss of life of civilians on all  sides  this is only a demonstration of the  intensity  of the azerbaijani offenses but there's  also a degree of asymmetry here  we're uh we're in a devastating round of  revenge and retribution  unfortunately this is why an immediate  ceasefire is an imperative  an immediate unconditional cease-fire  unfortunately  turkey and its patron azerbaijan  refused to abide by this offering  opportunity  but in many ways it's the civilians who  bear the brunt of this  war and at the same time we need to  actually  return to diplomacy force of arms is  not a solution richard you're  you're calling for a return to diplomacy  which is totally understandable given  this situation  but it's clear that there's a tit for  tat going on here  um with both sides lobbying accusations  at the other  shouldn't you be able to concede that an  attack that  took place at the night where there are  civilians uh very close by  is in fact not a good look for armenia  oh i would go even further it's  unacceptable let's be honest  it has followed several days of  indiscriminate shelling and bombardment  of civilian population centers  in nagorno-karabakh an expansion of the  fighting into armenia proper but this  second attack on ganja in no way should  be excused  by seeking a military target in other  words  the use of locating military  areas within civilian vicinities is  deplorable  but moreover this has to end now  no side is completely immune 